es government advances. Gross principal alternative retained in KP10 | Not secured-debt covenant leverage or agency-adjusted leverage; carrying vs principal matters | pp.105,126\\u2013132,148 |\\n| Finance-lease wording | P.131 describes 262/193 as \\u201cnet of the current portion\\u201d; detailed p.148 clearly splits 6+256 and 5+188 with totals 262/193 | Use p.148 current/noncurrent detail; retain p.131 wording inconsistency rather than adding 262 again to long-term debt | pp.131,148 |\\n| Maturity schedule | Retain p.133 aggregate schedule and individual debt labels separately; p.133 2027 bucket does not evidently accommodate notes labeled due 2027 | Total arithmetic passes, annual allocation remains unresolved; no synthetic corrected schedule | pp.126,133 |\\n| Dividends declared vs paid | IS21 per-share declaration, EQ23 equity charge, CF28 cash payments and BS14 accrued payable are distinct measures; vested/unvested equivalents complicate comparison | Do not infer common dividends alone from CF28, or charge the October payment to FY2024 cash flow | pp.105\\u2013109,146 |\\n\\n### Evidence-based financial read-through\\n\\n**Operating performance.** TransDigm's annual sales rose from IS01 FY2023 6,585 to FY2024 7,940; C06 calculates a 1355 increase and a 0.205771 growth fraction. Ordinary issuer EBITDA rose from EB05 3,148 to 3,813, while EBITDA As Defined rose from NB01 3,395 to 4,173; C06 calculates the latter increase at 778 and growth fraction 0.229161. Operating margins were broadly stable across FY2023\\u2013FY2024 (KP12), despite higher S&A and intangible amortization (IS04\\u201305). The complete below-gross-profit block is those two rows, not COGS or total cash-flow D&A (C01). Management attributes gross-margin improvement to its operating strategy and overhead absorption (p.36), higher S&A compensation to option valuation and increased acquisition activity (p.37), and amortization growth to acquisitions (p.37). These are source-attributed explanations; no independently proven price/volume split or constant-perimeter earnings bridge is supplied.\\n\\n**Cash conversion.** CFO increased from CF22 1,375 to 2,045 (C06 increase 670). CFO/EBITDA As Defined increased across the annual columns (KP14), and the CFO-minus-capex proxy increased from 1236 to 1880 (KP07). Receivables and inventory consumed cash in each annual column (CF15\\u201316), while FY2024 income-tax working balances consumed cash versus a FY2023 inflow (CF17); the full CF01\\u201321 reconciliation is in C18. This is a comparison of reported components, not an assertion that acquisition balance-sheet movements equal organic working-capital cash flows. Cash taxes rose (CF44) even though GAAP/current tax provisions are different measurements (IS11/CB04). CF28 distributions and CF24 acquisitions are outside the selected FCF proxy, so it is not residual cash available after all deployment. Source p.52 expressly warns that non-GAAP earnings exclude important cash obligations.\\n\\n**Funding and liquidity.** CF38 shows positive FY2024 financing cash flow versus a FY2023 outflow; CF30\\u201336 preserve the issuance/repayment composition. Cash rose (CF42), but debt carrying amount also rose (KP09). Gross carrying-basis leverage edged higher (KP15), whereas cash-netted leverage declined (KP16); the latter must not be read as unrestricted economic liquidity. In particular, BS14 FY2024 dividends payable 4,216 is an accrued distribution, not freely deployable cash. P.131 states that fourth-quarter note and term-loan proceeds, together with cash, funded the special-dividend declaration; p.146 records its subsequent payment. Historical expense-net coverage improved on the selected basis (KP17), but it is not cash-interest or covenant coverage.\\n\\n**Non-debt funding liabilities reviewed.** All BS11\\u201320 liabilities were reviewed, together with stockholders' deficit BS21\\u201328; deficit is equity presentation, not a funding liability. Material non-debt details include BS14 dividends payable, BS15 accrued/other current liabilities, BS18 deferred taxes and BS19 other non-current liabilities in both supplied instants. P.125 breaks BS15 into compensation/benefits 256/217, interest 185/125, dividend equivalents 180/19, contract liabilities 168/79, income taxes 108/138, loss contract reserves 50/42, warranties 36/28, environmental/litigation 27/16, operating leases 19/16, FX forwards 1/5 and other 186/169 (FY2024/FY2023; FUND01\\u201311). These are operating/accrual obligations, not automatically debt or excess liquidity. Contract-liability growth is attributed to acquisitions and advances (p.122). BS18 is a deferred accounting tax liability, distinct from cash taxes. BS19 includes noncurrent lease and compensation obligations: stock-based compensation liability 102/48 per p.143 and dividend equivalents 47 at FY2024 per p.146; a complete BS19 decomposition was not established. BS13 accounts payable is operating supplier funding, not reclassified debt. These observations support liability awareness, not an exhaustive maturity/liquidity model.\\n\\n**Subsequent-event segregation.** SE01: October 2024 securitization draw 162.5 (p.131). SE02: October 2024 payment of special dividend 4,216 and dividend equivalents 132 (p.146). Both are post-period disclosures; neither modifies BS01/BS12 or FY2024 CF22/CF28. P.146 reports FY2024 common special dividends totaling 6,153, with 1,937 paid in the first quarter and 4,216 accrued at year-end; vested dividend equivalents 233/38/86 are separate (EPS03). Historical funding read-through can identify the accrued cash call without rolling October activity backward.\\n\\n## Calculations\\n\\n### T4.9 \\u2014 Calculation register and operand conventions\\n\\nAll new arithmetic was executed with the supplied Decimal-38 sum/ratio kernel. Tables below separate calculations from reported facts. Period order in vector results is FY2024, FY2023, FY2022 unless specified. Every amount operand refers to the 