rospace cycles, not broad industrial diversification. | Description/country/sector stated here once; no peer ranking. |\\n| B02 Product mix | Documentary Fact | S1 p.5, Segments: Power & Control supplies \\u201cmechanical/electromechanical actuators and controls, ignition systems and engine technology, specialized pumps and valves\\u201d; Airframe includes \\u201cengineered latching and locking devices\\u201d, \\u201ccockpit security components and systems\\u201d, displays, restraints, parachutes and testing systems; Non-aviation includes ground restraints, space actuators, gas-turbine controls and heavy-equipment refueling. Financial anchors: U1 SG04, SG08, SG09. | Numerous components and applications spread individual product exposure; still predominantly one sector \\u2192 diversification within aerospace does not eliminate common demand shocks. | No product-level revenue percentages disclosed; measure = null for product-family mix. Segment totals are not individual product shares. |\\n| B03 Revenue-channel estimate | Management Language | S1 p.4: \\u201cWe estimate that approximately 90% of our net sales for fiscal year 2024 were generated by proprietary products\\u201d; \\u201capproximately 55% ... were generated from the aftermarket\\u201d. S1 p.7, Customers: commercial/general aviation aftermarket \\u201capproximately 31%\\u201d; commercial aerospace OEM \\u201capproximately 27%\\u201d; defense including OEM and aftermarket \\u201capproximately 40%\\u201d; non-aerospace \\u201capproximately 2%\\u201d. | Proprietary installed components create repeat replacement opportunities; OEM placement seeds later parts sales \\u2192 revenue durability may exceed the initial production run but remains utilization-dependent. | Estimates, not audited channel margins. The total aftermarket estimate includes military; defense is not additive to total aftermarket. U1 SG02/SG06 are commercial/non-aerospace aftermarket, not all aftermarket. |\\n| B04 Aftermarket margin/stability